{"event":"changes_requested","job_id":"in-person-guides-offline-transactions","reason":"Full first-attempt review independently read the complete pinned raw and verified SHA-256 0bd5f29ad995299a998ff4c593d379fe8022572cf8899973f3a38d011746f171 against the trusted order. The accepted receipt, candidate and suggestions agree, the canonical URL and raw provenance are correct, all five quotes are exact at their stated locations, and the detail locators resolve to the described material. The entry correctly preserves merchant liability and merchant-owned floor-limit controls; the local-network, static-IP and mutual-TLS prerequisites; the firmware qualification; reader-local collection/storage versus cloud-final transaction status; the five-day storage, post-upload 24-hour context-query and daily-reconciliation windows; and the boundary that collection, storage or upload is not authorization, settlement or funding proof. Routine certificate commands, response bodies, approximate capacity, cancellation mechanics and other detailed values remain appropriately in the fully read raw. The root index to braintree-index to braintree-payment-platform route exists, and the reciprocal proposal is relevant and sufficiently qualified. Two bounded material issues remain. First, the prerequisite takeaway omits the required provider-side certificate provisioning step: the raw says the root CA, reader certificate and reader private key must be sent to Braintree support so the reader can download provisioned certificates at startup. A reader asking 'Can I enable offline transactions solely by putting the POS and reader on a static local network and generating mutual-TLS certificates?' can infer yes from the candidate's apparently complete prerequisite list; the generic 'reader provisioning details' locator does not expose the external Braintree-support dependency in the retrieval answer. Second, 'Offline requests ... include deferredAuthorization: true' is overbroad. The captured page applies that variable to offline transaction requests/examples, while its supported-offline list also includes non-transaction reader interactions such as text display, line-item display and receipt printing. A reader asking whether every offline reader API call needs deferredAuthorization would receive an unsupported yes; the later supported-calls locator cannot cure the universal retained claim. Both issues can be corrected narrowly without changing evidence, routes or the approved shared update."}
